## 3. Direct logarithmic Kronecker-limit assembly at the actual caller

Set k=2/sqrt(d)>0. The lattices D_c^sigma represent all ideal classes
exactly once: their classes are the base class times c. For
Lambda=v*b, the summand |lambda|^2/(k*area(Lambda)) is
|Norm(y)|/Norm(b) with y in b. Each nonzero y determines an integral
ideal (y)*b^-1, with precisely two generators per ideal because
d>=7 and the unit group is {+1,-1}. The ideal classes thus partition
the sum with multiplicity two (equivalently hasSum_ideal_classes).
For s>1, absolute convergence permits regrouping:
  sum_c F_c(s) = 2*(s-1)*zeta(s)*L(s,chi_K).
Here F_c is EXACTLY the normalized lattice series in kronecker_limit.
The retained KLF says F_c(1)=pi*k and right derivative
  pi*k*(2gamma_E-log(A_c/(k*pi^2))-(1/6)*log D'_c).
By continuity at 1, the Euler product identity gives
2*L(1,chi_K)=h*pi*k>0. From the ordinary zeta Laurent expansion
(s-1)*zeta(s)=1+gamma_E*(s-1)+o(s-1), and differentiability of
L at 1, the right derivative of the same sum divided by h*pi*k
is gamma_E+Re L'/L(1,chi_K).
Equating the derivatives (finite sum over classes) yields
  (1/h)*sum_c [log A_c + (1/6)*log D'_c]
    = log(k*pi^2)+gamma_E-Re L'/L(1,chi_K).
Insert (1) and exponentiate:
  area(C^sigma)*|sigma(w)|^2
   = pi*exp(gamma_E-Re L'/L(1,chi_K))*(2*pi/sqrt(d)).   (2)
This is the precise chowla_selberg normalization, not merely
a classical period proportionality. The entire ideal norm dependence
has cancelled; all sigma share one w, with local norms one.
